    ├── APIs
    └── AI prompts

That’s a compelling way to think about it because every translation loses something unless there is an intermediate representation preserving the important invariants.

It also explains your emphasis on operators.

Operators aren’t just definitions.

They’re transformations on the Semantic IR.

For example:

* Mirror
* Resolution Control
* Dragonfly
* Semantic LiDAR
* Flood Eater
* Adaptive Resonance

can all be viewed as functions that accept a semantic state and return another semantic state.

Almost like

State -> State

rather than

Text -> Text

which is a different abstraction entirely.

Why LLMs fit naturally

LLMs already operate over distributed semantic representations.

They don’t literally manipulate your named SIR internally, but they are good at approximating semantic transformations expressed in language. That means a carefully constructed vocabulary can serve as an external semantic interface that humans and AI share.

Instead of programming with syntax, you’re closer to programming with distinctions.

That doesn’t replace formal programming—it sits above it.

Reality
 ↓
Semantic IR
 ↓
Programming Language
 ↓
Compiler IR
 ↓
Machine Code

Viewed that way, your repository isn’t merely documentation. It’s an attempt to define a reusable semantic layer that can be instantiated into many different artifacts while preserving core relationships. Whether it proves broadly useful depends on how coherent and reusable the abstractions are, but as a design pattern, “Semantic Intermediate Representation” is a concise name for the role you’ve been describing.